            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>1. Pre-Audit Preparation</h2>
<p>Before starting the compliance audit, operations staff need to complete the following preparations:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Tool checklist</strong>: Log into each advertising platform backend (Meta Business, Google Ads, TikTok Ads, etc.) to ensure normal account permissions; prepare the compliance checklist template;</li>
<li><strong>Historical data retrieval</strong>: Export the past 7 days of ad performance data, including ad review status, rejection reasons, and account warning notifications;</li>
<li><strong>Team notification</strong>: If major compliance issues are found, inform finance and campaign managers in advance to avoid launching new high-risk campaigns during the audit period.</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Note</strong>: If the account has received a platform warning email, be sure to screenshot and archive the email, and prioritize handling the warning matters before performing routine audits.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>2. Basic Account Inspection (Daily Must-Do)</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Login Security Verification</h3>
<ul>
<li>Check if there are abnormal login records (unknown IPs or devices) on the admin account linked to the account;</li>
<li>Confirm that two-factor authentication (2FA) is enabled and backup verification methods are valid.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Step 2: Account Balance and Recharge Status</h3>
<ul>
<li>Check if the account balance is sufficient to avoid service interruption due to overdue payment;</li>
<li>Check for any unreceived recharge records; if abnormal, contact the account manager for verification.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Step 3: Policy Announcement Review</h3>
<ul>
<li>Log into each platform's official policy page to check for new restricted categories or creative requirements;</li>
<li>Focus on: Industry-specific regulations updates for regulated industries (finance, healthcare, gaming).</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>3. Ad Creative Compliance Inspection</h2>
<h3>Inspection Checklist:</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Check Item</th>
<th>Qualified Standard</th>
<th>Common Violation Cases</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Image text ratio</td>
<td>No more than 20%</td>
<td>Promotional prices occupying over 1/3 of main image</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Clickbait/ misleading claims</td>
<td>No exaggerated promises</td>
<td>"Click to get" "100% win rate" phrases</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Brand logo</td>
<td>Fixed position, compliant size</td>
<td>Logo obscuring core content or too small</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Landing page consistency</td>
<td>Creative and landing page info match</td>
<td>Ad shows Product A but redirects to Product B page</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Sensitive elements</td>
<td>No nudity, violence, political content</td>
<td>Background showing controversial patterns</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Audit Execution Process:</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Batch screening</strong>: Use the platform's built-in ad preview tool to check the creative status of each running ad;</li>
<li><strong>Issue marking</strong>: After finding suspected non-compliant creatives, immediately record the ad ID, violation type, and discovery time in the checklist;</li>
<li><strong>Priority sorting</strong>: High-risk issues (involving account security) are prioritized, medium-risk issues (affecting delivery performance) come second, low-risk issues (optimization suggestions) can be scheduled into weekly plans.</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>4. Delivery Behavior Monitoring</h2>
<h3>Frequency Requirements:</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Daily</strong>: Check for ad review status updates (any new rejections);</li>
<li><strong>Weekly</strong>: Analyze if delivery frequency is too high (easily triggers platform traffic limits), if landing page loading speed is normal;</li>
<li><strong>Monthly</strong>: Review overall compliance performance, track rejection rate trends, identify recurring problem types.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Abnormal Signal Identification:</h3>
<ul>
<li>If the same ad group is rejected 3+ times consecutively, consider pausing delivery and redesigning creatives;</li>
<li>If an account shows high spend but no conversions, it may be due to traffic mismatch after creative rejection; investigate immediately;</li>
<li>When receiving "account restricted" notifications, do not resubmit similar creatives repeatedly to avoid aggravating the penalty.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>5. Exception Handling and Reporting</h2>
<h3>Handling Principles:</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Record on discovery</strong>: Any compliance anomalies must be entered into the audit log, including time, platform, account ID, and problem description;</li>
<li><strong>Quick response</strong>: High-risk issues (such as possible account suspension) must be notified to team leaders within 1 hour;</li>
<li><strong>Closed-loop tracking</strong>: After issues are resolved, fill in the handling results for future review.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Common Issue Responses:</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Creative rejection</strong>: Check the specific reason given by the platform, modify according to policy documents, and resubmit;</li>
<li><strong>Account suspension</strong>: Organize appeal materials (business license, delivery qualification proof, original creative files, etc.) and submit appeals through official channels;</li>
<li><strong>Recharge not received</strong>: Keep recharge receipt screenshots and contact agent or platform customer service for verification.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Conclusion</h2>
<p>Compliance auditing is not a one-time task but requires a normalized mechanism. It is recommended that teams print and post this SOP in a visible location at workstations, so new employees can quickly get started. Through standardized processes, teams can effectively reduce account risks and ensure the continuity and stability of overseas advertising campaigns.</p>
